<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="curcumin glucoside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4421" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Curcumin (dihydroferuloylmethane) is a yellow pigment found in turmeric (|FRAME:TAX-136217|). It is used in many ways, as an ingredient in food such as a colorant of spices (e.g. curry powder) and mustard. Curcumin also has a long history in the traditional medicine of Asian countries. Recently, curcumin has been investigated with respect to its potent anti-inflammatory and ant-leishmanial properties |CITS: [11878200]|, anti-cancer activity and a possible clinical application for treatment of Alzheimer&apos;s disease (|CITS:[18573304]| and referenced in |CITS: [15178322]|). Although curcumin offers a significant potential as a novel medicine or lead compound its bioavailability is dramatically decreased due to the extremely limited solubility of curcumin aglycones in watery solutions. However, it has been found that the solubility of curcumin can be enhanced considerably by means of glucosylation |CITS:[14644434]|. Hence the pharmacological exploration of those compounds is considered a major attempt to further explore the practical applications of pharmacologically useful curcumin glucosides |CITS: [15178322]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The pathway displayed here reflects a more applied aspect of secondary plant metabolism. Although curcumin has been metabolized in |FRAME:TAX-4058| the compound and its derivatives have not been reported in this plant. The occurrence of curcumin and metabolites thereof are mainly restricted to monocots, more specifically to the order of Zingiberales |CITS: [GANG06]|. Therefore the glucosyltransferases identified in |FRAME:TAX-4058| may also be involved in the detoxification of xenobiotics |CITS:[15926195]| which is supported by the up-regulation of enzyme activity after treatment with |FRAME:CPD1F-2| |CITS:[17940060]|. Testing 10 different plant cell suspension cultures |FRAME:TAX-4058| turned out to be the only one to produce a wide variety of glucosylated curcumin species. Curcumin was not only converted to simple glucosides by introducing glucose residues onto the two phenolic hydroxyl groups (curcumin monoglucoside - CMG, curcumine diglucoside - CDG) but also by forming L-1,6-glycosidic linkages, leading to the gentiobioside derivatives (curcumin gentiobioside - CGB, curcumin gentiobiosyl glucoside - CGG, curcumin digentiobioside - CDGB). The corresponding curcumin glucoside 1,6-glucosyltransferase has been purified and biochemically characterized |CITS:[17940060]|. In addition to that, the flavonoid 1,6-glucosyltransferase catalyzing the sequential elongation of the gentioside chain to form not only gentiobioside but also gentiotrioside and gentiotetraside of |FRAME:CPD-6602| has been cloned from |FRAME:TAX-4058| and the catalytic properties of the recombinant protein determined |CITS:[19561332]| The solubility of the curcumin glucosides has been enhanced dramatically. The solubility increased 230-fold for CMG, 10.000-fold for CDG and reached over 20 million-fold for CDGB compared to the curcumin aglycone |CITS: [14644434]|. The glucosides obtained by an enzymatic reaction in the cell cultures have been much more effective in terms of solubility than other approaches, e.g. the formation of cyclodextrin complexes of curcumin which resulted in a 26.000-fold solubility increase |CITS: [12204572]|. A UDPG:glucosyltransferase (UGT) was identified in |FRAME:TAX-4058| (CaUGT2) that was able to carry out the first two glucosylations to the curcumin mono and diglucoside in a sequential manner |CITS: [15178322]|. The other glucosylation steps have not yet been assigned to a special UGT. The substrate specificity of CaUGT2 was relatively broad glucosylating esculetin as well. However, the formation of a diglucoside of esculetin was not detected. Therefore, it was proposed that CaUGT2 may possess certain position specificity for the hydroxyl group to be glucosylated |CITS: [15178322]|. The expression of the CaUGT2 was rapidly induced under the influence of methyl jasmonate and salicylic acid. It was proposed that the UDPG:glucosyltransferase of |FRAME:TAX-4058| may represent a defense related enzyme involved in the detoxification of xenobiotica and/or biosynthesis of defense molecules.</Description>
